diff --git a/docs/system/arm/pic32cm-jh01-cpro.rst b/docs/system/arm/pic32cm-jh01-cpro.rst
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..766894f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/system/arm/pic32cm-jh01-cpro.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,114 @@
+Microchip PIC32CM JH01 Curiosity Pro (``pic32cm-jh01-cpro``)
+=============================================================
+
+.. note::
+   Peripheral Pretender: a personal project in a downstream fork
+   of QEMU. AI-assisted code; not for submission to upstream QEMU.
+
+The PIC32CM JH01 Curiosity Pro evaluation kit (EV81X90A) carries a
+PIC32CM5164JH01100 microcontroller: an Arm Cortex-M0+ with 512 KiB of
+flash, 8 KiB of data flash and 64 KiB of SRAM, a 32 MHz crystal on
+XIN/XOUT and a 32.768 kHz crystal on XIN32/XOUT32.
+
+The SoC model (``pic32cm5164jh01100-soc``) is built from a description
+generated from the device's ATDF file (``generator/atdf2qemu.py`` in the
+Peripheral Pretender project): the memory map, every peripheral instance
+with its register tables and reset values, the NVIC lines and the GCLK
+channel of each peripheral. Peripherals are QOM children of
+``/machine/soc`` named after their instance (``/machine/soc/gclk``,
+``/machine/soc/sercom4``, ...).
+
+Supported devices
+"""""""""""""""""
+
+- Cortex-M0 core (QEMU has no Cortex-M0+) reporting the Cortex-M0+ r0p1
+  CPUID 0x410CC601, NVIC with 32 interrupts and 2 priority bits, SysTick
+  clocked from the CPU clock
+- Flash, data flash (both read as erased, 0xFF, where no image is
+  loaded), SRAM, and the NVM rows at 0x00800000-0x0080FFFF (calibration,
+  user row with the production fuse values, OTP rows, serial number)
+- Clock system: OSCCTRL (XOSC, OSC48M, FDPLL96M), OSC32KCTRL (XOSC32K,
+  OSC32K, OSCULP32K), GCLK generators and peripheral channels, MCLK CPU
+  divider. Oscillators are ready as soon as they are enabled and every
+  synchronization is instant. Frequencies propagate through QEMU clocks
+  to the CPU clock (4 MHz after reset, 48 MHz after the usual
+  OSC48MDIV = 0) and to each peripheral's ``gclk`` clock input.
+- NVMCTRL: PARAM, READY, CTRLB, command keys (PROGE without the key)
+- SUPC: BODVDD loaded from the user row, its ready flags
+- RSTC: RCAUSE is POR at power-up, SYST after AIRCR.SYSRESETREQ, WDT after
+  a watchdog reset and EXT after any other reset (for example
+  ``system_reset`` in the monitor); SRAM keeps its contents across them
+- WDT: CTRLA, CONFIG and EWCTRL loaded from the user row; a wrong CLEAR key
+  resets the system
+- PM: SLEEPCFG and STDBYCFG
+- PAC: WRCTRL bookkeeping in STATUSx with the data sheet's error rules
+- DSU: device ID (revision set by the SoC property ``silicon-rev``), the
+  DSU_EXT view at 0x41002100 and the CoreSight ROM table
+- DIVAS: signed and unsigned division and square root, also through the
+  IOBUS view at 0x60000200
+- Every other peripheral instance is a generic register block: reset
+  values, store and read back, write-1-to-clear flags, set/clear
+  registers, SYNCBUSY reading 0, software reset through CTRLA.SWRST and
+  the register view selected by the mode field (SERCOM, TC, RTC). Writes
+  to it are reported with ``-d unimp`` as ``<instance>.<REGISTER>: write
+  0x... not modelled``. Interrupt lines follow ``INTFLAG & INTENSET``,
+  with OR gates for the shared vectors.
+
+Missing devices
+"""""""""""""""
+
+- SERCOM and PORT are generic register blocks, so there is no UART output
+  yet: firmware waiting for SERCOM INTFLAG.DRE does not progress. LED0
+  (PC05) and SW0 (PB19) are not connected.
+- The MPU: QEMU only gives Armv7-M cores MPU regions, so the
+  ``mpu-ns-regions`` setting of 8 has no effect and MPU_TYPE reads 0.
+  CONTROL.nPRIV is not implemented on the Cortex-M0 either.
+- Watchdog time-out, PAC write protection, flash programming through
+  NVMCTRL, DSU CRC32 and chip erase, sleep modes and every other
+  peripheral behaviour (DMAC, EIC, TC, TCC, RTC, ADC, DAC, AC, CAN, ...).
+
+Board and SoC properties
+""""""""""""""""""""""""
+
+The board connects ``-serial`` options to SERCOM4 (EDBG virtual COM
+port), SERCOM2 (EXT1), SERCOM3 (EXT2), SERCOM6 (EXT3) and SERCOM0 (LIN), in
+that order, once SERCOM has a UART model.
+
+The SoC takes two properties, which can be given with ``-global``:
+
+``silicon-rev``
+  ``B0`` (default) or ``C0``, shown in DSU.DID.REVISION.
+
+``serial-number``
+  32 hex digits: the words at 0x0080A00C, 0x0080A040, 0x0080A044 and
+  0x0080A048, in that order. The default is
+  ``50500000000000010000000200000003``.
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+  $ qemu-system-arm -M pic32cm-jh01-cpro -kernel app.elf \
+      -global pic32cm5164jh01100-soc.silicon-rev=C0
+
+Boot options
+""""""""""""
+
+Load an ELF with ``-kernel``, or an Intel HEX file with ``-device
+loader,file=app.hex``. Words that an image places in the user row
+(0x00804000) replace the production values and take effect at reset, as
+the WDT, SUPC and NVMCTRL.LOCK reset values are loaded from them.
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+  $ qemu-system-arm -M pic32cm-jh01-cpro -kernel app.elf \
+      -display none -serial stdio -d unimp,guest_errors
+
+``-d unimp,guest_errors`` lists the register writes the model does not
+implement and the accesses the device would reject. The trace events
+``mchp_clk_update``, ``mchp_regfile_read``, ``mchp_regfile_write``,
+``mchp_regblock_unimp_write`` and ``mchp_rstc_reset_request`` show the
+clock tree, every register access and reset requests. ``info qtree`` in
+the monitor shows the frequency of every clock, for example the
+``cpuclk`` input of ``armv7m``.
+
+Run release builds of the firmware: QEMU stops with ``cpu_abort`` when a
+fault handler executes ``BKPT`` (lockup is not modelled).
